@flexiarg snatch/play-the-authored-order-first
@title Play the Authored Order First
@audience policy authors
@tone practical
@style pattern
@why snatch/have-a-temperament

! conclusion: Name the identity temperament — the empty edit — and report every other temperament's score as a difference from it.

  + context: **Policy grain.** Operand: the precedence field, left unchanged.
    The instance is `patterns-overrides {}` (`checks/playout_snatch.clj:160`),
    the empty override map that `pattern-policy` falls through to the authored
    `:precedence` of each pattern.

  + IF:
    You have a cascade with an authored precedence and are considering a
    temperament that re-wires it.

  + HOWEVER:
    An empty edit looks like no policy at all, so it goes unnamed — and once it
    is unnamed there is nothing for a re-wiring to be measured against. Every
    claim that a temperament helps is a difference, and a difference needs two
    terms.

  + THEN:
    Record the identity temperament as the run's temperament when it is the one
    in force, exactly as a non-empty one would be recorded, and state every
    other temperament's result as a delta against it on the same scenarios.

  + BECAUSE:
    S-G4 — that the score must depend on the wiring — is not satisfiable by one
    wiring, which is a theorem and not a preference:
    `mathlib4/DarkTower/WarMachine/PolicyGrade.lean:61`
    `wiringSensitive_needs_two_wirings`, *"a score cannot be sensitive to a
    choice of wiring that does not exist"* (`:59-60`). The identity is the
    cheapest available second term and it is already in the harness.
    + evidence: a second domain reached the same element independently —
      `library/ants`'s `baseline-cyber-ant` carries an empty `@aif-delta`,
      described in `futon2/holes/cascade-ants.edn:48` as *"the identity element
      of the pattern algebra"*.
